{"subjectId":"python","id":"debugging-profiling","title":"Debugging & Profiling","group":"Testing & Tooling","order":63,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Debugging finds <em>why</em> code is wrong; profiling finds <em>where</em> it is slow or hungry. Both start with reading carefully and measuring, not guessing.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-alert\"></i>Reading a traceback</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Traceback</figcaption><pre><code>Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"report.py\", line 12, in &lt;module&gt;\n    print(average(scores))\n          ~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^\n  File \"report.py\", line 4, in average\n    return sum(xs) / len(xs)\n           ~~~~~~~~^~~~~~~~~\nZeroDivisionError: division by zero</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Read the last line first</div><div class=\"step-desc\">The exception type and message: <em>what</em> went wrong.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Walk up the stack</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Frames are listed oldest → newest; the last frame is where it was raised.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Find your code</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Skip library frames; the lowest frame in <em>your</em> file is usually the place to fix.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Use the markers</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Since 3.11 <code>~~~^^^</code> points at the exact sub-expression that failed.</div></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-dots\"></i>print, logging and assertions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>items = [3, 5]\ntotal = sum(items)\nprint(f\"{items=} {total=}\")          # items=[3, 5] total=8   ← the \"=\" specifier labels values\nprint(f\"{total / len(items)=:.2f}\")  # total / len(items)=4.00</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-pause\"></i>pdb — the built-in debugger</h3>\n<p>Put <code>breakpoint()</code> where you want to stop (3.7+). The program pauses there with an interactive <code>(Pdb)</code> prompt; you can inspect variables, step line by line and continue.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def apply_discount(price, pct):\n    breakpoint()              # execution pauses here\n    return price - price * pct / 100</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>pdb session</figcaption><pre><code>&gt; shop.py(3)apply_discount()\n-&gt; return price - price * pct / 100\n(Pdb) p price, pct\n(200, 15)\n(Pdb) n\n--Return--\n(Pdb) c</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p expr</code> / <code>pp expr</code></td><td>Print / pretty-print an expression</td></tr><tr><td><code>n</code> (next)</td><td>Run the current line, stepping <em>over</em> calls</td></tr><tr><td><code>s</code> (step)</td><td>Step <em>into</em> a function call</td></tr><tr><td><code>c</code> (continue)</td><td>Run until the next breakpoint</td></tr><tr><td><code>r</code> (return)</td><td>Run until the current function returns</td></tr><tr><td><code>l</code> / <code>ll</code></td><td>List source around the current line / the whole function</td></tr><tr><td><code>w</code> (where)</td><td>Show the call stack</td></tr><tr><td><code>u</code> / <code>d</code></td><td>Move up / down the stack to inspect callers</td></tr><tr><td><code>b file:line</code> / <code>b func</code></td><td>Set a breakpoint</td></tr><tr><td><code>q</code></td><td>Quit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python -m pdb script.py                 # start under the debugger\n$ python -m pdb -c continue script.py     # run; drop into pdb post-mortem on a crash\n$ PYTHONBREAKPOINT=0 python script.py     # disable every breakpoint() call</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>IDE debuggers (VS Code, PyCharm) do the same with clicks: breakpoints, conditional breakpoints, watch expressions. <code>faulthandler</code> (<code>python -X faulthandler</code>) dumps tracebacks even on hard crashes and hangs.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>timeit — micro-benchmarks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import timeit\n\nsetup = \"data = list(range(10_000)); s = set(data)\"\nlist_time = timeit.timeit(\"9_999 in data\", setup=setup, number=1_000)\nset_time = timeit.timeit(\"9_999 in s\", setup=setup, number=1_000)\nprint(set_time &lt; list_time)          # True   ← O(1) hash lookup beats an O(n) scan</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python -m timeit -s \"xs = list(range(1000))\" \"sum(xs)\"\n20000 loops, best of 5: 11.2 usec per loop</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>cProfile — where does the time go?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import cProfile, pstats, io\n\ndef slow_square(n):\n    return sum(i * i for i in range(n))\n\ndef work():\n    return [slow_square(20_000) for _ in range(20)]\n\nprofiler = cProfile.Profile()\nprofiler.enable()\nwork()\nprofiler.disable()\n\nout = io.StringIO()\nstats = pstats.Stats(profiler, stream=out).sort_stats(\"cumulative\")\nstats.print_stats(5)\nprint(\"slow_square\" in out.getvalue())     # True</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Typical report (timings vary)</figcaption><pre><code>      400063 function calls in 0.041 seconds\n\nOrdered by: cumulative time\n\nncalls  tottime  percall  cumtime  percall filename:lineno(function)\n     1    0.000    0.000    0.041    0.041 app.py:6(work)\n    20    0.000    0.000    0.041    0.002 app.py:3(slow_square)\n    20    0.019    0.001    0.041    0.002 {built-in method builtins.sum}\n400020    0.022    0.000    0.022    0.000 app.py:4(&lt;genexpr&gt;)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ncalls</code></td><td>How many times the function was called</td></tr><tr><td><code>tottime</code></td><td>Time spent in the function itself, excluding sub-calls</td></tr><tr><td><code>cumtime</code></td><td>Time including everything it called — sort by this to find hot paths</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python -m cProfile -s cumtime app.py      # profile a whole script\n$ python -m cProfile -o out.prof app.py &amp;&amp; snakeviz out.prof   # visualise</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>tracemalloc — where does the memory go?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import tracemalloc\n\ntracemalloc.start()\nbig = [str(i) * 10 for i in range(50_000)]\ncurrent, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()\ntop = tracemalloc.take_snapshot().statistics(\"lineno\")[0]\ntracemalloc.stop()\nprint(peak &gt; 1_000_000, top.size &gt; 1_000_000)   # True True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Kind</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cProfile</code></td><td>Deterministic, built in</td><td>Which functions take the time</td></tr><tr><td><code>py-spy</code></td><td>Sampling, attaches to a running process</td><td>Production processes, no code changes</td></tr><tr><td><code>line_profiler</code></td><td>Line by line</td><td>The slow line inside a known-slow function</td></tr><tr><td><code>scalene</code></td><td>CPU + memory + GPU sampling</td><td>An all-in-one view</td></tr><tr><td><code>memray</code> / <code>tracemalloc</code></td><td>Memory allocations</td><td>Leaks and peak usage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you find why a Python program is slow?</summary><div><p>Reproduce with a realistic input, profile with <code>cProfile</code> (or <code>py-spy</code> on a live process), sort by cumulative time, then zoom in with <code>line_profiler</code> or <code>timeit</code>. Fix the biggest hotspot — usually an algorithm or data-structure issue — and measure again.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does breakpoint() do?</summary><div><p>It calls <code>sys.breakpointhook()</code>, which starts <code>pdb</code> at that line by default. The <code>PYTHONBREAKPOINT</code> environment variable can switch to another debugger or disable it (<code>=0</code>).</p></div></details></section>"}